<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zaria Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1808 CE – 1902 CE · capital Zaria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Fulani emirate that replaced the Hausa kingdom of Zazzau, a southern frontier of the Sokoto Caliphate and a major source of slaves.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallam-musa">Mallam Musa</a>, a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-zazzau">Kingdom of Zazzau</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> ruler fled south and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abuja-emirate">Abuja Emirate</a>. Musa became the first Fulani emir of Zaria, ruling until 1821.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The emirship rotated among several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> dynasties, the Mallawa, Bornawa, Katsinawa and later Sullubawa, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> confirming each choice, a system that caused frequent rivalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a> was one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> in area, and its southern frontier with the peoples of the Nigerian Middle Belt was a zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> that supplied labour and tribute for Sokoto.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a> in 1902, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Kwassau was deposed in 1903 for resisting British authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, and the Emir of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-zazzau">Zazzau</a> still reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zaria-emirate.jpg" alt="Zaria Emirate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Aminucrus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zazzau_Emirs_Palace_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1808 CE – 1902 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mallam Musa, Kwassau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Zazzau, Zazzau Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=zaria-emirate" aria-label="Zaria Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 1765 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zaria Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 2021L5212 2021M5212 2021L5144 1947M5212 2021L5170 2068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5212 2021h0M5144 1947h0M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 2021h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 2021h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=zaria-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
